        "math_academy_id": "q-115213",
        "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1.$",
        "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 1 \\\\ {(0.62)}^{x+5}\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061[(0.62)^{x+5}] & \\le \\operatorname{ln}\u20614 \\\\ (x+5)\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.62 & \\le 2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.62$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.62} \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20610.62}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
        "deliberately_incorrect_submission": false,
